// Primitive Data Types
/* Integral Numbers :-
        1. byte.
            This is the smallest integer type. It’s useful when you are working with a stream of data from
             a network or file,or when you are very concerned about saving memory in large arrays.
                 Default value:- 0
                 Size (Bits) :- 8-bit.
                 Minimum Value :- -128.
                 Maximum Value :-  127.

        2. short.
            short is mostly a vestige of older computer architectures. In modern Java,
            it’s rarely used because the performance gain over an int is negligible on most systems.
               Default value:- 0
               Size (Bits) :- 16-bit.
               Minimum Value :- -32,768.
               Maximum Value :-  32,767.
        3. int.
                    This is the gold standard. Unless you have a specific reason to do otherwise, use int for whole numbers.
                    It is large enough for most calculations and is optimized for modern processors.
                               Default value:- 0
                               Size (Bits) :- 32-bit.
                               Minimum Value :- -2( power to -31) (i.e. -2147483648).
                               Maximum Value :- 2( power to -31) - 1 (~2.1 billion)  (i.e. 2147483647).


        4. long.
                Use this when int isn't big enough (e.g., measuring the distance between stars in kilometers
                 or tracking world population).

                Note: When assigning a value to a long, you should append an L to
                the number so Java knows it's a long literal:

                long distance = 9000000000L;

                       Default value:- 0
                       Size (Bits) :- 64-bit.
                       Minimum Value :- -2( power to 63) (i.e. -9223372036854775808).
                       Maximum Value :-  2( power to 63) - 1  (i.e. 9223372036854775807).


         */

        /* Decimal Numbers :-
        1. float.
        2. double.
         */

        /* Characters :-
            1. char
         */

        /*  Booleans
            1. booleans.
         */